-------------------------------------
How to build the LambdaMOO+foo server
-------------------------------------

Part 1: System requirements

You will need a modern UNIX or UNIX-like Operating System in order to use this
code. In addition, you will require the following be installed on that system:

* The GCC compiler suite; other compilers may work, but they are untested.
* A recent version of bison, or a pre-installed version of yacc.
* A recent version of gperf

Part 2: Compiling the server

* 'cd' to the directory you put the source code (example: /tmp/LambdaMOO-plus-foo)
* Create the build directory with 'mkdir build'
* Go to the build directory with 'cd build'
* Configure the build with 'cmake ..'
* Build it with 'make'
* This should result in a file called 'moo', which is the MOO server binary.

Part 3: Making the proper installation directories

* Create a directory outside of the mooserver source directory where you
  want your MOO to reside.
* Copy the 'moo' binary from the build directory into that directory, along
  with restart.sh.
* Create subdirectories there called files-FUP and files-FIOs
  (parts of the server need these directories)

Part 4: Making it work

* Grab whatever core you want to start with (or use Minimal.db if you're truly
  adventurous), and copy it to the directory you created in Part 3.

* Once you've got a .db file ready to use, you can start the MOO running in the
  background with 'sh restart.sh <dbname> <port>' (note: do not include the
 ".db" in dbname)
